    33	## What the paper claims
    34	
    35	The stage card (`stage_116.tex` line 15-17) states verbatim: "First D/N mixed half-wave fixes \(L_W=\pi a\sqrt{(1+r_c)/3}/2\) and the bare outgoing scale." The notes (authoritative, more detailed) lay out four concrete deliverables: (1) the D/N half-wave eigenvalue `k_W = π/(2L_W)`, `Ω_W = π c_s/(2L_W)`; (2) the bare even coefficient `κ_0 = (ω²/Ω_W²)/z² = 4L_W²/(π²a²)` derived from the eigenfunction problem `q''+k²q=0, q(0)=0, q'(L_W)=0`; (3) the compensation-selected tube length `L_W = πa/2 · sqrt((1+r_c)/3)` enforced by `κ_0 = (1+r_c)/3` with `r_c = λ²/(K_s K_q)`; (4) the bare outgoing form `D_W^bare(z) = (1+r_c)(1 - z²/3 - i z⁵/9) + O(z⁶)` and γ_0 = (1+r_c)/9; (5) after Stage-97 (1+r_c) renormalization, the canonical coefficients `κ_c = 1/3`, `γ_c = 1/9`.
    36	
    37	## What the script claims to verify
    38	
    39	The SymPy script defines `r_c = lam²/(K_s K_q)`, hardcodes `kappa0_from_tube = 4L_W²/(π²a²)`, solves the compensation equation `kappa0_from_tube = (1+r_c)/3` for `L_W` (printing the result, but with no assertion), then defines `kappa0_bare = (1+r_c)/3` and divides by `(1+r_c)` and asserts the quotient equals `1/3`. Same pattern for `γ_c`. Finally, it constructs `D_bare = (1+r_c)·(canonical-form)`, divides by `(1+r_c)`, and asserts the quotient equals the canonical form. The Mathematica script mirrors this structure, but adds one additional substantive assertion: `lWRequired - πa·sqrt((1+r_c)/3)/2 == 0` (the actual tube-length law of paper claim 3).
    40	
