    47	| Tube-length law `L_W = πa√((1+r_c)/3)/2` | SymPy: only prints `L_W_required`, no assertion. Mathematica: `expectZero["tube-length law", ...]` (wl:39) | partial (only Mathematica covers it) |
    48	| `γ_0 = (1+r_c)/9` literally appears | Defined as literal `gamma0_bare = (1+r_c)/9` (sympy:36; wl:42) | extra/tautological scaffolding |
    49	| Renormalization: `(1+r_c)·canonical` ÷ `(1+r_c)` = canonical | Both engines assert this via tautological constructions | match-but-tautological |
    50	| Final values `κ_c = 1/3, γ_c = 1/9` | Both engines assert `kappa_c - 1/3 == 0`, `gamma_c - 1/9 == 0` | tautological (algebraic identity of construction) |
    51	
    52	`paper_alignment: partial` — the central paper claim 3 (tube-length law) is exercised on the Mathematica side but not on the SymPy side. The two interior claims of the stage (κ_0 derivation from eigenfunction, then κ_c emerging from renormalization) are not substantively verified: κ_0 is hardcoded and κ_c follows tautologically.
